[COMMENTS]
STATION,Velocity area station in a substantially straight reach with natural shoal control. Three major PWS abstractions in the catchment have a substantial effect on the flow regime. GENERAL: Velocity area station, 35m wide, in a substantially straight reach with natural shoal control. Llanymynech gauging section frequently changes shape due to the movement of the bed as a result of sediment loads deposited in the channel following high flow events. Gravel bars are often formed and have remained stable enough to become heavily vegetated in the past, these can cause skew flow. The physical changes to the channel control the stage-discharge relationship. STATION HYDROMETRY: Daily flow rating relatively stable. Out-of-bank flows gauged from a cableway extension over the floodplain. Flow gauged above bank full. Multiple peak flow ratings applied across period of record. Rating changes due to bed shift. At highest flows a small amount of water bypasses the cableway on the left bank - flow estimated. FLOW RECORD: Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in September 2019 (WINFAP Files v8). Ratings reviewed in 2022 and data reprocessed for the full period of record. FLOW REGIME: Three major PWS abstractions in the catchment have a substantial effect on the flow regime, notably the supply exported to Liverpool from Lake Vyrnwy.
CATCHMENT,Steep headwater streams and broad-bottomed valleys; storage in valley sands and gravels. Predominantly Silurian and Ordovician sandstones and mudstones overlain by Boulder Clay and sands and gravels in the main tributaries. Land use is moorland, forestry and grazing.
Qmed Suitability,Gauged above QMED.
Pooling Suitability,Gauged beyond QMED. Rating determined above bank full, confirmed by gaugings.
[END]
